ed into Item 2.02 of this Current Report on Form 8-K by reference. The information in the preceding paragraphs, as well as Exhibits 99.1 and 99.2, shall not be deemed &#8220;filed&#8221; for purposes of Section 18 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (the &#8220;Exchange Act&#8221;), or otherwise subject to the liabilities o
10941|ASTI|2026-05-27|historical_scan|HIGH|38.4|13.48|routine|none|floor||1.00|09:35|Historical scan: 13.5% gap, 7.6x rvol||No concrete, verifiable catalyst was found to explain the gap — gap+volume alone is never sufficient for HIGH. The move appears driven by speculative retail interest, solar sector drift, or short-squeeze dynamics rather than a company-specific event. The setup matches neither active narrative cohort (defense UAS, nuclear-for-AI), so no theme/narrative axis fires.|No news was found to explain the pre-market gap-up in ASTI. Without a concrete, verifiable catalyst — such as an earnings release with specific figures, a major contract announcement, or an FDA/regulatory decision — the move cannot be attributed to a company-specific event. ASTI is a small-cap/penny-stock solar technology company that is susceptible to gap-ups driven by speculative retail interest, sector momentum, or short-squeeze dynamics rather than fundamental catalysts.|
10942|QFIN|2026-05-27|historical_scan|HIGH|68.4|10.41|strong|MODERATE|floor||1.00|09:35|Historical scan: 10.4% gap, 7.7x rvol||The catalyst is a concrete Q1 earnings beat (Adj. EPS $1.12 vs $0.96, revenue $566.7M vs $539.5M), but it is a backward-looking quarterly print with no guidance raise and is undercut by deteriorating fundamentals — loan facilitation volume down 26.8% YoY and outstanding balance down 18.5% YoY. Earnings beats sit at the bottom of the Pradeep hierarchy and there is no theme/narrative alignment (Chinese Credit-Tech matches neither the drone nor nuclear cohort). A shrinking-business beat on a $1.8B name does not justify HIGH; this is a routine EPS-beat gap, lifted only modestly by the numeric conc|QFIN reported Q1 2026 earnings with a concrete beat on both top and bottom lines: Adj. EPS of $1.12 vs. $0.96 estimate (~17% beat) and revenue of $566.7M vs. $539.5M estimate (~5% beat). The catalyst is verifiable with specific numbers from the SEC 6-K filing and Benzinga report. However, the report also showed notable operational headwinds — total loan facilitation volume declined 26.8% YoY and outstanding loan balance fell 18.5% YoY — and no explicit forward guidance raise was mentioned, tempering the classification from game_changer to strong.|[SEC 6-K filed 2026-05-27, items ] EX-99.1 2 tm2615601d1_ex99-1.htm EXHIBIT 99.1 Exhibit 99.1 Qfin Holdings Announces First Quarter 2026 Unaudited Financial Results Shanghai, China, May 26, 2026, Qfin Holdings, Inc. (NASDAQ: QFIN; HKEx: 3660) (&ldquo;Qfin Holdings&rdquo; or the &ldquo;Company&rdquo;), a leading AI-empowered Credit-Tech platform in China, today announced its unaudited financial results for the first quarter ended March 31, 2026. First Quarter 2026 Business Highlights &middot; As of March 31, 2026, our platform has connected 167 financial institutional partners and 297.5 million consumers *1 with potential credit needs, cumulatively, an increase of 11.0% from 268.2 million a year ago. &middot; Cumulative users with approved credit lines *2 were 64.8 million as of March 31, 2026, an increase of 10.9% from 58.4 million as of March 31, 2025. &middot; Cumulative borrowers with successful drawdown, including repeat borrowers was 39.5 million as of March 31, 2026, an increase of 11.0% from 35.5 million as of March 31, 2025. &middot; In the first quarter of 2026, financial institutional partners originated 12,925,120 loans *3 through our platform. &middot; Total facilitation and origination loan volume *4 was RMB65,034 million, a decrease of 26.8% from RMB88,883 million in the same period of 2025. RMB31,417 million of such loan volume was under capital-light model, Intelligence Credit Engine (&ldquo;ICE&rdquo;) and total technology solutions *5 , a decrease of 28.3% from RMB43,811 million in the same period of 2025. &middot; Total outstanding loan balance *6 was RMB114,387 million as of March 31, 2026, a decrease of 18.5% from RMB140,273 million as of March 31, 2025. RMB55,664 million of such loan balance was under capital-light model, &ldquo;ICE&rdquo; and total technology solutions, a decrease of 29.3% from RMB78,681 million as of March 31, 2025. &middot; The weighted average contractual tenor of loans originated by financial institutions across our platform in the first quarter of 2026 was approximately 11.3 months, compared with 10.2 months in the same period of 2025. &middot; 90 day+ delinquency rate *7 of loans originated by financial institutions across our platform was 3.50% as of March 31, 2026. &middot; Repeat borrower contribution *8 of loans originated by financial institutions across our platform for the first quarter of 2026 was 86.8%. 1 Refers to cumulative registered users across our platform. 2 &ldquo;Cumulative users with approved credit lines&rdquo; refers to the total number of users who had submitted their credit applications and were approved with a credit line at the end of each period. 3 Including 774,739 loans across &ldquo;V-pocket&rdquo;, and 12,150,381 loans across other products. 4 Refers to the total principal amount of loans facilitated and originated during the given period. 5 &ldquo;ICE&rdquo; is an open platform primarily on our &ldquo;Qifu Jietiao&rdquo; APP (previously known as &ldquo;360 Jietiao&rdquo;
10742|RCAT|2026-05-28|live|HIGH|100.8|16.07|strong||||1.20|07:00|RCAT’s recent gap up is being driven by a cluster of bullish defense and technology catalysts: a new Japan military drone contract, the acquisition of Quaze Technologies for autonomous wireless charging, and fresh Buy-rated analyst coverage with a price target well above the prior trading range. Key current catalysts behind the move: - Japan defense contract: Red Cat won a competitive contract fro|||Red Cat Holdings is gapping up on multiple concrete catalysts: (1) H.C. Wainwright initiated coverage with a ~100% upside target and positive thesis on converting NATO/Asia-Pacific orders into recurring revenue, (2) secured its first international defense contract with Japan's Ministry of Defense for Black Widow drone systems, and (3) completed acquisition of Quaze Technologies for wireless power transfer capabilities. These represent a strong cluster of analyst validation, major geopolitical contract win, and strategic M&A that enhances the product portfolio for a previously underfollowed defense contractor.|
10743|KTOS|2026-05-28|live|MODERATE|64.8|10.54|strong||||1.20|07:00|The recent gap up in Kratos Defense & Security Solutions (KTOS) is being driven primarily by renewed interest in defense/dual‑use tech names and positioning ahead of the anticipated Anduril IPO, where KTOS is being traded as a sympathy play and beneficiary of capital rotation into defense and autonomous systems. Key current catalysts behind the move: 1) Anduril IPO sympathy and re‑rating of defens|||The primary catalyst for KTOS's gap-up appears to be sector momentum driven by the Trump Administration's reported support for domestic drone makers through Pentagon funding and equity deals, combined with Kratos's strategic $67 million Florida manufacturing expansion. While the news items lack specific earnings numbers or guidance raises, the convergence of favorable defense spending tailwinds, concrete capex investment, and industry-wide drone stock surge (ONDS, RCAT also mentioned) suggests a positive sentiment shift in the defense/drone sector that benefits KTOS as a key player.|
10744|AVAV|2026-05-28|live|HIGH|72.0|10.33|strong||||1.20|07:00|AVAV’s gap-up was most likely driven by a fresh defense-contract catalyst, not by the class-action headlines. The key news was AeroVironment’s announcement that it is expanding its Huntsville, Alabama missile facility with $20.2 million in government funding to accelerate production of its next-generation Freedom Eagle-1 interceptor, which directly supports the company’s defense growth narrative.[|||AeroVironment announced a $20.2 million government-backed expansion of its Huntsville, Alabama facility to accelerate production of next-generation counter-unmanned aircraft interceptor systems. This represents concrete government investment and commitment to long-term defense programs, combined with earlier news of a secured three-year, $43 million Department of War contract for expanded mission software capabilities. The catalyst is strong due to: (1) government capital backing for facility expansion, (2) commitment to established defense contracts, and (3) clear pathway to increased production capacity and revenue growth in the high-demand drone defense sector.|
10745|SNOW|2026-05-28|live|MODERATE|53.3|35.23|routine||||1.20|09:45|SNOW gapped up on a strong Q1 FY2027 earnings beat, a raised outlook, and a major AI-growth signal from the company’s expanded AWS collaboration. The biggest immediate catalyst was Snowflake’s earnings release, which showed non-GAAP EPS of $0.39 versus $0.32 expected and revenue of $1.39 billion versus $1.32 billion expected, alongside guidance that the market read as constructive.[3] The news flo|||Snowflake reported strong Q1 earnings, raised full-year product revenue guidance, and announced a transformative $6 billion multi-year spending deal with Amazon Web Services. This combination of a solid beat, guidance raise, and a major strategic partnership with a cloud giant represents a concrete, verifiable game-changer catalyst that justifies the significant pre-market gap-up.|
10746|PHR|2026-05-28|live|MODERATE|54.0|22.56|routine||||1.00|09:45|There is no fresh positive catalyst or news explaining a gap up in Phreesia Inc. (ticker: PHR); the most recent material development in 2026 is negative (a revenue cut and a related shareholder lawsuit), which previously drove the stock sharply lower rather than higher. Key recent catalyst: - On March 30, 2026, after the close, Phreesia issued significantly lowered revenue growth projections for f|||Phreesia reported Q1 2027 earnings with a 13% revenue increase, positive net income, a +150% earnings surprise (EPS beat of substantial magnitude), and a +0.51% revenue beat. The company demonstrated strong growth in payment solutions and profitability expansion driven by the AccessOne acquisition, which represents meaningful guidance momentum. This is a legitimate earnings beat with profitability improvement on a healthcare SaaS platform, warranting a "strong" classification.|
10747|KSS|2026-05-28|live|MODERATE|64.8|21.73|routine||||1.20|09:45|Kohl’s (KSS) most recent gap up has been driven primarily by a sharp shift in sentiment around its earnings trajectory and turnaround prospects, not by a single new M&A headline or one-off rumor. The key underlying catalysts behind the move: 1) Strong recent price and volume action tied to earnings re‑rating   KSS has dramatically outperformed the market over the last several months, with the stoc|||Kohl's reported Q1 CY2026 earnings that were in line with expectations (revenue met, 2% YoY sales decline to $3.17B, GAAP loss of $0.13/share vs. analyst expectations). While the stock jumped 11.2% in pre-market, this is an in-line earnings result with no guidance raise, no positive guidance revision, and no material improvement signaled—it's actually a mild miss (loss per share) relative to what appears to be analyst estimates. The gap-up lacks a clear positive catalyst; it may reflect relief that results weren't worse than feared, but this does not qualify as a meaningful earnings beat or guidance raise that would drive EP momentum.|
10943|SNOW|2026-05-28|historical_scan|HIGH|96.0|35.23|game_changer|HIGH|floor||1.00|09:35|Historical scan: 35.2% gap, 5.6x rvol||Concrete beat-and-raise quarter: product revenue +34% YoY ($1.33B), 126% NRR, RPO +38%, accelerating $1M+ customer adds, and explicitly raised FY guidance — a real, quantified catalyst (sales acceleration) that fully justifies HIGH on a $82B name. The AI tailwind narrative is genuine and company-specific, lighting the theme axis, though it does not join either listed cohort (defense drones / nuclear). I hold rather than promote to game_changer: this is a strong fundamental re-rate but not a structurally transformative single event, so the 35% gap reflects an elite earnings print rather than a |Snowflake reported Q1 FY2027 earnings with product revenue of $1.33B (+34% YoY) and total revenue of $1.39B (+33% YoY), which the CEO described as "the strongest sequential dollar growth in our history." The net revenue retention rate of 126%, 38% YoY growth in remaining performance obligations ($9.21B), and a surge in $1M+ customers (779, up 29% YoY — with 46 new additions vs. 26 a year ago) all signal strong acceleration. Critically, management raised full-year product revenue guidance, citing AI as a powerful and accelerating tailwind — making this a clear beat-and-raise quarter with a compelling AI-driven growth narrative.|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-05-27, items 2.02,9.01] snow-20260527 0001640147 false 0001640147 2026-05-27 2026-05-27 UNITED STATES SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION Washington, D.C. 20549 FORM 8-K CURRENT REPORT Pursuant to Section 13 or 15(d) of The Securities Exchange Act of 1934 Date of Report (Date of earliest event reported): May 27, 2026 SNOWFLAKE INC. (Exact name of registrant as specified in its charter) Delaware 001-39504 46-0636374 (State or other jurisdiction of incorporation or organization) (Commission File Number) (IRS Employer Identification No.) 135 Constitution Drive 94025 Menlo Park, California (Address of Principal E