‑to‑date market or news data on MMYT (MakeMyTrip) in the information you provided, so I cannot see the specific catalyst that caused today’s gap up. To pinpoint the latest driver, you should check, in order of likelihood: 1) Company‑specific news: - Most recent earnings release (EPS/Revenue beat, guidance raise, improved margin commentary, or strong booking trends). - Any new disclosure on India travel demand, hotel/airline partnership deals, or product launches (super app features, loyalty program changes, fintech/payment features). - Announcements about index inclusion (e.g., addition to a major index or ETF) or a broker upgrade/target price hike from a large global bank/Indian broker. 2) Sector / macro catalysts: - Positive data on Indian travel, tourism, or online travel penetration (e.g., record domestic air passengers, strong holiday season bookings). - India macro upgrades (GDP growth forecasts raised, foreign inflow into India tech/consumer internet basket). - Regulatory developments that favor online travel agents (reduced taxes/fees, clarity on regulations). 3) Flows and positioning: - A notable change in short interest or a short squeeze. - A large block trade or institutional accumulation (often visible in volume spikes and block prints on the tape, and later in 13F or bulk‑deal disclosures). - Inclusion in or rebalancing of a thematic ETF (India tech/consumer, EM internet, travel/leisure). Practically, to answer precisely “what caused today’s gap up” for MMYT, look at: - The time‑stamped company news feed (press releases, 6‑K/20‑F/earnings, exchange announcements). - Real‑time headline feeds from major financial terminals or newswires around the pre‑market/market open when the gap occurred. - Broker notes or upgrades published in the last 24–48 hours. - Unusual options and volume activity around the move. If you tell me the exact price move (gap size, pre‑market vs regular session) and any headline you’re seeing on your terminal or broker news feed, I can help you interpret whether the move is likely earnings‑driven, flow‑driven, or macro/sector‑driven.
