12312|LZB|2026-06-17|live|HIGH|54.0|26.50|routine|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|09:50|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-06-16, items 2.02,7.01,9.01] La-Z-Boy (LZB) is gapping up on the back of a strong fiscal Q4 earnings print that substantially beat expectations, showed clear margin expansion, and highlighted solid retail demand plus strategic progress. Key catalysts: 1) Big EPS beat and solid top line   - Fiscal Q4 2026 adjusted EPS came in at about $1.26 vs roughly $0.82 consensus, a very lar|LZB's fiscal Q4 2026 print shows a company-specific multi-quarter operational inflection—a massive EPS beat (+54% vs. consensus), margin expansion (+50 bps YoY), and strong retail segment written sales growth (+11%)—idiosyncratic to management execution and demand recovery, with no industry-wide the|Fresh, primary-sourced 8-K (filed 2026-06-16) carries a large adjusted EPS beat ($1.26 vs ~$0.82) backed by REAL operational substance — 50bps margin expansion and double-digit Retail written/delivered sales growth (+11%/+9%), so the EPS isn't a hollow print on a flat top line. As a mature consumer cyclical the load-bearing signal is the demand-confirmed margin inflection plus a $300M buyback that is meaningful against a ~$1.4-1.8B cap, justifying promotion above the routine floor. Lack of theme/cohort heat and the cyclical (non-growth) profile cap it at strong rather than game_changer.|La-Z-Boy reported Q4 FY2026 earnings on June 16, 2026, with adjusted diluted EPS of $1.26 and adjusted operating margin of 9.9% (up 50 bps YoY), alongside strong Retail segment written sales growth of +11% and delivered sales growth of +9%. The company also announced a new $300M share repurchase authorization (significant relative to its ~$1.4B market cap), completed strategic portfolio exits (American Drew/Kincaid), and demonstrated broad-based margin improvement. While this is a mature consumer cyclical (not a high-growth name), the combination of solid operational beats, meaningful margin expansion, a large buyback, and strategic cleanup initiatives across the business represents a clearly positive, company-specific catalyst above a routine in-line print.|
12313|AEHR|2026-06-17|live|MODERATE|70.0|15.87|strong|MODERATE|judge|theme|1.00|09:50|AEHR’s latest gap up has been driven primarily by sector-wide strength in semiconductor and AI-related names, with AEHR breaking to new 52‑week highs alongside Intel (INTC) and Marvell (MRVL), rather than by a single, stock‑specific headline like earnings or a new contract. Key points on the current move: 1) Group/sector catalyst   AEHR has been rallying as part of a broader move in semis and AI‑e|The gap-up is driven by sector-wide AI and semiconductor momentum (Intel, Marvell, and other chip/equipment names rallying in tandem to 52-week highs), not by an idiosyncratic company event; the follow-on production order is modest relative to market cap and is amplified BY—rather than independent o|The grounded corpus attributes the move to broad AI/semi sector strength and 52-week-high group action, not a verified stock-specific event; no direct primary source is present (has_direct_source=false). The analyst's claimed follow-on FOX-XP order is unquantified and, for a $3.6B name, a single-system production order is minor — not enough to load-bear a HIGH. With an unidentified/immaterial company catalyst and only sector tailwind, this is a routine-to-MODERATE setup, not a HIGH.|The concrete, fresh catalyst is a follow-on production order for a fully automated FOX-XP Wafer-Level Burn-In System, announced via Benzinga on 2026-06-17. While the web summary attributes the gap to broad AI/semiconductor sector momentum, the Benzinga headline confirms a specific, company-issued press release about a new customer order — this is a real, stock-specific catalyst. However, given AEHR's $3.3B market cap, a single follow-on production order for one system is unlikely to be transformative in magnitude, making this a "strong" rather than "game_changer" catalyst, especially when amplified by favorable sector tailwinds in AI/semis.|
12314|SWBI|2026-06-18|live|HIGH|70.0|15.59|strong|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.00|07:20|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-06-17, items 2.02,9.01] Smith & Wesson Brands (SWBI) has been gapping on relatively thin, headline‑light newsflow, so the most recent gap up is primarily a positioning/flow move rather than a clean, single-company fundamental catalyst. Here’s what is driving it: 1) Sector and macro tape   SWBI trades as a small‑cap cyclical/“security”/consumer‑discretionary hybrid, and its shar|Q4 FY2026 earnings show a company-specific multi-quarter inflection: 26.7% YoY revenue growth, nearly doubled EPS ($0.36 vs. $0.19 YoY), and margin expansion (gross margin +100bp, adjusted EBITDA margin at 17.3%)—all idiosyncratic earnings acceleration with no sector-wide theme, policy driver, or su|Fresh 8-K (items 2.02/9.01, filed 2026-06-17) confirms a real, dated earnings catalyst: net sales +26.7% YoY to $178.4M with gross-margin and EBITDAS expansion — revenue acceleration is the load-bearing signal for HIGH, satisfying both freshness and the growth-name rubric. The SEC corpus framing it as a flow move is contradicted by the primary earnings filing, so I lean on the verifiable revenue beat. Absence of an explicit guidance raise caps it below game_changer, keeping it strong at the floor.|SWBI reported Q4 FY2026 earnings with net sales of $178.4M, a 26.7% YoY increase, beating expectations with EPS of $0.36/share (vs. $0.19 in the prior year quarter — nearly doubling). Gross margin expanded to 29.8% from 28.8%, and Non-GAAP Adjusted EBITDAS improved to 17.3% of net sales. The catalyst is a fresh, concrete earnings release (8-K filed June 17, 2026) showing strong revenue acceleration and significant profitability improvement, though no explicit forward guidance raise was mentioned in the excerpt, which prevents a game_changer classification.|
12315|UUUU|2026-06-18|live|HIGH|72.0|12.36|game_changer|HIGH|judge|policy|1.20|08:00|UUUU gapped up because Energy Fuels said it expects to hit its full-year uranium production guidance by mid-year, which the market read as a strong operating update. The company also highlighted very low processing costs at White Mesa and reaffirmed its strategic push into rare earths and other critical minerals, which added to the bullish reaction.[1] The immediate catalyst was the production-gui|The primary structural driver is the $725M Pentagon loan agreement for rare earth separation—a federal government financing/subsidy commitment—which ranks above the secondary production-guidance beat in the Pradeep hierarchy; policy outranks sales_acceleration.|The load-bearing driver is the fresh June 18 Pentagon Office of Strategic Capital $725M conditional loan — a government-policy-tier catalyst (highest in the hierarchy) worth ~19% of the $3.8B cap, dated to the gap day and squarely on the hot domestic critical-minerals/rare-earth theme. While no direct SEC source is attached, the dated Benzinga wire establishes freshness and the materiality is unambiguous, justifying the game_changer floor. The stale June 11 uranium update is secondary supporting narrative, not the driver.|The primary and freshest catalyst is the June 18, 2026 announcement that Energy Fuels entered a $725M conditional loan agreement with the Pentagon's Office of Strategic Capital to expand rare earth separation and metallization — a transformative, government-backed deal representing roughly 19% of the company's $3.8B market cap, which is highly material. The secondary catalyst is the June 11 mid-year uranium update, where UUUU expects to hit its full-year uranium production guidance by mid-year (1.6M lbs from White Mesa Mill), which signals strong operational execution. Together, these represent a major government financing commitment plus a production beat narrative, making this a clear game-changer event.|[Benzinga 2026-06-18] Energy Fuels Enters $725M Conditional Loan Agreement With Pentagon's Office Of Strategic Capital To Expand Rare Earth Separazation And Metallization. [Benzinga 2026-06-12] Goldman Sachs Maintains Buy on Energy Fuels, Lowers Price Target to $21. [Benzinga 2026-06-11] Energy Fuels Issues Mid-Year Update On Its U.S. Uranium Segment; Expects To Commence Further Modifications To Existing Phase 1 REE Circuits At White Mesa Mill From July 2026. [Web summary] UUUU gapped up because Energy Fuels said it expects to hit its full-year uranium production guidance by mid-year, which the market read as a strong operating update. The company also highlighted very low processing costs at White Mesa and reaffirmed its strategic push into rare earths and other critical minerals, which added to the bullish reaction.[1] The immediate catalyst was the production-guidance announcement, with shares rising more than 4% in premarket trading after the update.[1] The company said it expects 1.6 million pounds of finished uranium production from January through June 2026 at its White Mesa Mill, and it flagged the possibility of resuming ore processing later in 2026 depending on market conditions and mine output.[1] A secondary narrative supporting the move is the broader critical-minerals and tungsten angle. Separate coverage noted Energy Fuels’ role in the U.S. tungsten supply-chain rebuild and the market’s focus on domestic critical-minerals names as policy pressure builds to reduce reliance on China.[2][5]
12316|MLTX|2026-06-22|live|HIGH|96.0|19.10|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|07:00|MoonLake Immunotherapeutics (MLTX) gapped up on the back of a major positive clinical update in its lead program plus increased event-driven interest. The latest concrete catalyst: on June 21 the company reported that its Phase 3 VELA program for hidradenitis suppurativa (HS) with lead drug sonelokimab delivered strong 52-week results, including 67.2% HiSCR75, 33.1% HiSCR100 and 26.0% IHS4-100, wi|Phase 3 VELA program 52-week efficacy and safety data readout for sonelokimab represents a transformative, company-specific clinical inflection that de-risks the lead asset, materially validates the commercial thesis, and establishes a concrete regulatory pathway (BLA by late September 2026) — a com|Fresh, dated (June 21–22, 2026) Phase 3 VELA 52-week readout with strong, clean efficacy (67.2% HiSCR75, no new safety signals) plus a concrete BLA timeline (late Sept 2026) and Investor Day — a multi-layered, company-defining catalyst for a $1.4B clinical-stage biotech that materially de-risks the lead asset. Successful late-stage data is the load-bearing driver and is the strongest catalyst class for a pre-revenue biotech, fully justifying the HIGH/game_changer floor. Direct primary source is absent but the catalyst is corroborated across wire (Benzinga) and dated to today/overnight, so fres|The specific catalyst is the Phase 3 VELA program 52-week data readout (reported June 21–22, 2026) for sonelokimab in moderate-to-severe hidradenitis suppurativa, showing 67.2% HiSCR75, 33.1% HiSCR100, and 26.0% IHS4-100 with no new safety signals — strong, clean late-stage efficacy results. This is paired with a concrete regulatory timeline (BLA filing planned by late September 2026) and an Investor Day on June 22, making it a fresh, multi-layered catalyst package. For a $1.4B clinical-stage biotech, a successful Phase 3 readout with high response rates, clean safety, and a clear path to BLA filing is a transformative, company-defining event that materially de-risks the lead asset and justifies a significant re-rating.|[Benzinga 2026-06-22] Reported Sunday, MoonLake Announces Phase 3 VELA Program Week 52 Results Showing 67.2% Of Sonelokimab-Treated Patients Achieving HiSCR75 In Moderate-To-Severe Hidradenitis Suppurativa. [Web summary] MoonLake Immunotherapeutics (MLTX) gapped up on the back of a major positive clinical update in its lead program plus increased event-driven interest. The latest concrete catalyst: on June 21 the company reported that its Phase 3 VELA program for hidradenitis suppurativa (HS) with lead drug sonelokimab delivered strong 52-week results, including 67.2% HiSCR75, 33.1% HiSCR100 and 26.0% IHS4-100, with no new safety signals. The company also flagged an Investor Day for June 22 and guided to a planned HS BLA filing by late September 2026. That package of data (clean safety, high response rates, and a clear regulatory timeline) is a classic late‑stage biotech re‑rating catalyst and explains the gap move you’re seeing. More broadly, MLTX has been trading as a late‑stage trial/optionality story around sonelokimab across inflammatory indications, and traders have been positioning around these milestones, so the positive VELA readout plus near‑term BLA path is what specifically drove the latest leg higher and the gap up.
12317|SYRE|2026-06-22|live|HIGH|60.0|12.35|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.00|07:00|The specific catalyst is concrete and fresh: Spyre Therapeutics announced on June 15, 2026 that the 12-week induction data from Part A of its Phase 2 SKYLINE study of SPY002 in active Ulcerative Colitis met its primary endpoint, with SPY002 reported as well tolerated and showing a consistent safety profile. For a clinical-stage biotech with no approved products, a Phase 2 trial meeting its primary|SYRE's Phase 2 SKYLINE study achieving its primary endpoint for SPY002 in ulcerative colitis is a company-specific, binary clinical inflection that de-risks the lead asset and validates pipeline thesis—a material, idiosyncratic catalyst distinct from theme/policy/shortage drivers.|Fresh, dated (2026-06-15) Benzinga wire reports Spyre's Phase 2 SKYLINE study of SPY002 in ulcerative colitis MET its primary endpoint with clean safety — for a clinical-stage biotech with no approved products this is a transformative, binary de-risking event for the lead asset, exactly the type of catalyst that earns HIGH/game_changer. The vague web summary claiming "no hard catalyst" is overridden by the primary-source headline, which directly explains the 12% gap on 11x RVOL. Active theme participation plus a clean positive readout justify promotion above the MODERATE floor.|The specific catalyst is concrete and fresh: Spyre Therapeutics announced on June 15, 2026 that the 12-week induction data from Part A of its Phase 2 SKYLINE study of SPY002 in active Ulcerative Colitis met its primary endpoint, with SPY002 reported as well tolerated and showing a consistent safety profile. For a clinical-stage biotech with no approved products, a Phase 2 trial meeting its primary endpoint is a transformative, binary catalyst — it de-risks the lead asset significantly and validates the entire pipeline thesis. This is a clear, company-specific, verifiable press release event (not a generic web summary) that directly explains the gap, and its magnitude relative to SYRE's $7.7B market cap warrants a game-changer classification.|[Benzinga 2026-06-15] Spyre Therapeutics Announces 12-Week Induction Data From Part A Of Phase 2 SKYLINE Study Of SPY002 To Treat Active Ulcerative Colitis Meets Primary Endpoint; SPY002 Well Tolerated With Consistent Safety Profile. [Web summary] SYRE’s latest gap up was driven by stock-specific news and order flow, not a broad market event, but there is no single, clearly reported fundamental catalyst (like earnings, M&A, or a major contract) disclosed in the usual news feeds at the time of the move. Based on what is available, the gap looks more like a positioning/technicals plus sentiment-driven move than a response to a new, material corporate announcement. Key points for you as a trader/analyst: 1) No fresh, headline fundamental catalyst   There is no widely disseminated press release or 8‑K‑type event (earnings, guidance raise, M&A, strategic partnership, regulatory decision, or index inclusion) tied directly to the gap in SYRE. That strongly suggests the move was not triggered by a classic hard catalyst but by flows and positioning around existing themes in the name. 2) Likely drivers: positioning + sentiment   In the absence of a hard news item, a gap up in a smaller or mid-cap growth/biotech/AI-type name typically comes from a mix of: - Short-covering into/through a technical level (prior resistance, recent high, or key moving average).   - Momentum/quant flows chasing a recent multi-day uptrend or relative strength.   - Options-driven price action (market makers hedging into concentrated call buying can push the underlying higher pre-market).   - Sector sympathy: if a closely related peer with similar exposure (same therapeutic area, same tech niche, or same theme like AI, EV, or gene editing) posted good news, SYRE can gap in sympathy even without its own filing. Given the tape lately, the strongest candidate is a combination of momentum/quant flows and shorts caught leaning the wrong way, rather than a new RNS/PR. 3) How to verify in your tools   Since you’re a market analyst, the fastest way to confirm the above on your desk is: - Check the company’s press release feed and most recent filings around the open where the gap occurred. No new item timestamped before the gap = no hard catalyst.   - Look at pre‑market time & sales and Level 2: was there a cluster of prints at successively higher prices on relatively low liquidity, or a large opening auction imbalance? That pattern is consistent with a gap driven by order imbalance rather than news.   - Check broker/desk notes and options activity: an uptick in short-dated OTM calls and skew steepening into the move would support a flow/positioning explanation.   - Compare SYRE’s pre‑market move to a tight peer basket: if peers also gapped (even if less), you’re likely seeing a sector/“theme” impulse plus stock-specific illiquidity. 4) How to frame this in a note   For a trading/PM audience, you could characterize it along the lines of: “SYRE gapped higher on flow/technical factors rather 
