12345|SG|2026-07-17|live|none|56.4|15.16|routine|none|judge|unknown|1.20|09:50|If you mean SGX: the gap-up was most likely driven by a broad Singapore market breakout, not a company-specific surprise. The local market has been hitting record highs on heavy institutional buying, with gains concentrated in financials and other blue chips, while softer US inflation data and expectations of a less aggressive Fed supported risk appetite. [2][4] For SGX specifically, the latest ma|The provided catalyst narrative conflates SG (Sweetgreen, a consumer cyclical company) with SGX (Singapore Exchange); no verifiable company-specific driver for Sweetgreen could be identified, and the gap-up appears attributable to either a data-retrieval failure or a market-wide/sector rotation move|The corpus contains only wrong-company noise about SGX (Singapore Exchange), not Sweetgreen (SG) — no fresh, dated, company-specific catalyst exists and no direct source is present. With driver UNIDENTIFIED, the gap+volume alone is merely the market's vote and can never earn HIGH. Absent any verifiable Sweetgreen event, this defaults to routine below the MODERATE floor.|No concrete, verifiable company-specific catalyst could be identified for Sweetgreen (SG). The web search results returned information about SGX (Singapore Exchange), an entirely different company, indicating the news retrieval failed to surface any relevant Sweetgreen-specific news. With no earnings figures, guidance raise, analyst upgrades, or major contract disclosable for Sweetgreen, and no fresh dated catalyst present, the gap-up driver remains unidentified. Per trading rules, an unidentified or non-company-specific move defaults to "routine."|[Web summary] If you mean SGX: the gap-up was most likely driven by a broad Singapore market breakout, not a company-specific surprise. The local market has been hitting record highs on heavy institutional buying, with gains concentrated in financials and other blue chips, while softer US inflation data and expectations of a less aggressive Fed supported risk appetite. [2][4] For SGX specifically, the latest market narrative points to continued strength in exchange volumes and operating leverage, helped by the equity market development programme inflows, monetary easing expectations, and ongoing trading activity in Singapore equities. [9] If you mean a different “SG” ticker, tell me the exchange and company name, because SG can refer to multiple stocks.
12346|HUT|2026-07-20|live|HIGH|72.0|11.54|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|07:05|The fresh catalyst today (2026-07-20) is Hut 8's announcement of a 15-year, $9.8B capacity lease for 352MW at the second phase of its Beacon Point (Texas) data center campus — a triple-net, take-or-pay contract with a high-investment-grade tenant. At $9.8B in contracted base-term revenue, this deal alone represents roughly 95% of Hut 8's current $10.3B market cap, making it extraordinarily materia|Multi-year, multi-billion-dollar capacity lease represents a company-specific revenue inflection and transformative earnings rerating that is idiosyncratic to Hut 8's operational trajectory and fundamental financial profile, not a sector-wide theme, policy, or supply dynamic.|Fresh, dated-today (2026-07-20) Benzinga wire confirms a NEW 15-year, $9.8B take-or-pay capacity lease for Beacon Point Phase 2 — that single deal is ~95% of the $10.3B market cap, extraordinarily material and structurally transformative into a long-duration AI infrastructure landlord. Freshness is established by a primary wire despite has_direct_source=false, and the AI data center theme plus clean rerating narrative reinforce all three fire axes. This clears the REAL+MATERIAL bar for HIGH, promoting above the MODERATE floor to game_changer.|The fresh catalyst today (2026-07-20) is Hut 8's announcement of a 15-year, $9.8B capacity lease for 352MW at the second phase of its Beacon Point (Texas) data center campus — a triple-net, take-or-pay contract with a high-investment-grade tenant. At $9.8B in contracted base-term revenue, this deal alone represents roughly 95% of Hut 8's current $10.3B market cap, making it extraordinarily material relative to company size. Combined with the previously announced River Bend lease, Hut 8 now has ~$16.8B in long-duration AI infrastructure contracted revenue, fundamentally transforming the company's earnings profile and de-risking its growth runway — a clear structural rerating event.|[Benzinga 2026-07-20] Hut 8 Enters 15-Year, $9.8B Capacity Lease For 352MW On The Second Phase Of Its 1GW Beacon Point Data Center Campus In Texas. [Benzinga 2026-07-14] Benchmark Maintains Buy on Hut 8, Raises Price Target to $165. [Web summary] The latest gap up in HUT is being driven primarily by ongoing repricing of its large AI data center lease and financing deals, plus a recent bullish analyst price-target hike tied to those contracts, all against a backdrop of improving sentiment in AI infrastructure and crypto-linked names. More specifically, the key catalysts investors are still trading on are: • Massive long-term AI data center leases: Hut 8 has locked in two 15‑year, triple‑net, take‑or‑pay leases at its River Bend (Louisiana) and Beacon Point (Texas) campuses, totaling roughly 597 MW of IT capacity and about $16.8 billion in base‑term contracted revenue, with potential to reach over $40 billion if renewals are exercised. These deals effectively transform the company into a long‑duration AI infrastructure landlord and underpin the current AI‑driven rerating of the stock. • Beacon Point financing and investment‑grade notes: The company closed about $4.25 billion of senior secured notes for Beacon Point (investment‑grade Baa2), following roughly $3.25 billion raised for River Bend, giving Hut 8 around $7.5 billion of non‑dilutive project financing. This structure is viewed positively by equity holders since it funds the build‑out without issuing new shares. • Analyst price target raise: Benchmark recently lifted its price target to around $165, explicitly citing the scale and economics of the Beacon Point and River Bend leases and Hut 8’s pivot to AI‑focused data center infrastructure. The latest gap higher is consistent with the market continuing to digest that target increase and rerate the name upward. • AI infrastructure and sector sentiment: HUT is trading as part of the broader “AI data center” basket, alongside names like Cipher, with money rotating into operators that have long‑term, contracted AI capacity. This thematic flow is a material contributor to moves like the current gap. • Crypto/BTC beta and earnings focus: Hut 8 still has meaningful Bitcoin mining and digital‑asset exposure, so any stabilization or bounce in BTC helps sentiment in the name. Additionally, attention is building into the upcoming Q2 2026 earnings release in early August, where the market expects more color on Beacon Point and River Bend ramp, AI lease monetization, and guidance – that anticipation alone can drive pre‑earnings repricing. There does not appear to be a fresh, single‑day, company‑specific headline this morning; the gap up is more a continuation of investors repricing the stock around the AI lease/financing story, the recent aggressive analyst target, and improving sector sentiment rather than a brand‑new operating announcement.
12347|IREN|2026-07-20|live|HIGH|72.0|13.15|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|09:50|IREN announced $2.8 billion in new multi-year AI Cloud contracts with leading AI developers (including Microsoft, NVIDIA, Perplexity, Figure AI, and others), simultaneously raising its 2026 ARR target from $3.7B to over $4B — a massive guidance raise relative to a $14.1B market cap. The contracts include ~45% customer prepayments covering associated GPU capex, significantly de-risking future expan|IREN's $2.8B AI Cloud contract win with concrete prepayments and a 23% ARR guidance raise (2026 target: $3.7B→$4B+) represents a company-specific multi-quarter revenue inflection and beat-and-raise, idiosyncratic to IREN's execution and customer concentration—not a sector-wide AI infrastructure them|Fresh, primary-source 8-K/wire dated July 20, 2026 discloses $2.8bn in new multi-year AI Cloud contracts and a hard ARR guidance RAISE from $3.7bn to over $4bn (~85% now contracted) — the load-bearing revenue-acceleration signal for a growth name, not a mere EPS beat. Relative to a $14.1B cap this is transformative: contracted ARR now approaches ~30% of market cap with 4-year durability and 45% customer prepayments de-risking capex, sitting squarely in the hottest AI-compute theme. Freshness and materiality are both established via a direct filing, so I promote above the MODERATE floor to HIGH|IREN announced $2.8 billion in new multi-year AI Cloud contracts with leading AI developers (including Microsoft, NVIDIA, Perplexity, Figure AI, and others), simultaneously raising its 2026 ARR target from $3.7B to over $4B — a massive guidance raise relative to a $14.1B market cap. The contracts include ~45% customer prepayments covering associated GPU capex, significantly de-risking future expansion, and the weighted average contract term is ~4 years, signaling durable, high-quality revenue. This is a fresh, specific, numerically detailed press release dated July 20, 2026, representing a transformative acceleration in contracted revenue that materially re-rates the growth trajectory of the business.|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-07-20, items 2.02,9.01] false 0001878848 00-0000000 0001878848 2026-07-20 2026-07-20 UNITED STATES SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION Washington, D.C. 20549 Form 8-K CURRENT REPORT Pursuant to Section 13 or 15(d) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 Date of Report (Date of earliest event reported): July 20, 2026 IREN LIMITED (Exact name of registrant as specified in its charter) Commission File Number: 001-41072 Australia Not Applicable (State or other jurisdiction of incorporation) (IRS Employer Identification No.) Level 5, 55 Market Street , Sydney , NSW 2000 Australia (Address of principal executive offices, including zip code) + 61 2 7906 8301 (Registrant&#8217;s telephone number, including area code) (Former name or former address, if changed since last report) Check the appropriate box below if the Form 8-K filing is intended to simultaneously satisfy the filing obligation of the registrant under any of the following provisions: &#9744; Written communications pursuant to Rule 425 under the Securities Act (17 CFR 230.425) &#9744; Soliciting material pursuant to Rule 14a-12 under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14a-12) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 14d-2(b) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14d-2(b)) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 13e-4(c) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.13e-4(c)) Securities Registered Pursuant to Section 12(b) of the Act: Title of each class Trading symbol(s) Name of each exchange on which registered Ordinary shares, no par value IREN The Nasdaq Stock Market LLC Indicate by check mark whether the registrant is an emerging growth company as defined in Rule 405 of the Securities Act of 1933 (&#167;230.405 of this chapter) or Rule 12b-2 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (&#167;240.12b-2 of this chapter). Emerging growth company &#9744; If an emerging growth c