12364|EME|2026-07-30|live|HIGH|60.0|10.56|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.00|07:55|EMCOR reported Q2 2026 EPS of $9.06, massively beating the $7.21 estimate (+25.7%), on revenue of $5.154B vs. $4.709B expected (+9.5% beat). More critically, the company raised FY2026 adjusted EPS guidance to $32.00–$33.25 from $28.25–$29.75 (vs. consensus of $29.31), a ~12% raise at the midpoint, and raised revenue guidance to $20.0B–$20.5B from $18.5B–$19.25B, blowing past the $18.997B Street es|EMCOR's dual beat-and-raise on Q2 earnings and FY2026 guidance (+25.7% EPS beat, +12% guidance raise at midpoint) represents a company-specific multi-quarter revenue and earnings inflection driven by accelerating AI/data center infrastructure demand, not a broad sector-wide theme shared uniformly by|Fresh, primary-wire dated 2026-07-30: Q2 dual beat (revenue +9.5%, sales $5.154B) PLUS a large FY2026 guidance RAISE on both revenue ($20.0-20.5B vs $18.5-19.25B prior) and EPS — the load-bearing revenue-acceleration-plus-raise signal that pays on growth names, driven by structural AI/data-center backlog. The undated web-summary AI narrative is corroborative, not the driver; the actual catalyst is the today's beat-and-raise. Materiality is genuinely large in absolute terms for a $29.9B cap and the theme (AI infra picks-and-shovels) is hot, so game_changer/HIGH holds.|EMCOR reported Q2 2026 EPS of $9.06, massively beating the $7.21 estimate (+25.7%), on revenue of $5.154B vs. $4.709B expected (+9.5% beat). More critically, the company raised FY2026 adjusted EPS guidance to $32.00–$33.25 from $28.25–$29.75 (vs. consensus of $29.31), a ~12% raise at the midpoint, and raised revenue guidance to $20.0B–$20.5B from $18.5B–$19.25B, blowing past the $18.997B Street estimate. This is a dual beat-and-raise of exceptional magnitude — the EPS guidance raise alone is roughly $3.50+ above prior midpoint — driven by structural AI/data center infrastructure tailwinds, making this a textbook game-changer catalyst for an EP setup.|[Benzinga 2026-07-30] EMCOR Group Raises FY2026 Adj EPS Guidance from $28.25-$29.75 to $32.00-$33.25 vs $29.31 Est; Raises FY2026 Sales Guidance from $18.500B-$19.250B to $20.000B-$20.500B vs $18.997B Est. [Benzinga 2026-07-30] EMCOR Group Q2 EPS $9.06 Beats $7.21 Estimate, Sales $5.154B Beat $4.709B Estimate. [Web summary] The most recent meaningful catalyst for EME’s upside gap has been a combination of very strong earnings tied to AI/data center demand and growing institutional interest in that story, rather than a single discrete headline like M&A or regulatory news. Here’s the setup behind the gap higher: 1) Fundamental driver: blowout earnings and AI/data center backlog   EMCOR has been putting up record numbers, with Q1 2026 revenue around $4.63B, up nearly 20% year over year, driven heavily by U.S. Electrical and Mechanical Construction tied to data centers and infrastructure. That print beat consensus and highlighted a large, diversified backlog leveraged to AI/data center build‑out and industrial/infrastructure projects. The market has increasingly re‑rated EME as a key picks‑and‑shovels name for AI infrastructure, which is the structural backdrop supporting gap‑ups on positive flow. 2) Backlog and narrative shift   Commentary over the last few months has focused on EMCOR becoming a “go‑to builder” for data centers, with record Remaining Performance Obligations (RPOs) and strong margins in electrical and mechanical segments. As investors rotated into AI infrastructure beneficiaries, EME’s story went from cyclical contractor to secular growth play, which tends to produce gaps when incremental good news or positioning shifts hit a relatively tight float. 3) Institutional and positioning catalysts   Recent news flow has highlighted sizeable new institutional positions and adjustments in EMCOR – for example, multi‑million dollar stakes disclosed by various asset managers and funds, combined with optimism about data center cooling and electrical build‑outs. Those filings don’t always trigger a gap by themselves, but they reinforce the narrative, support higher targets, and can catalyze squeeze‑type moves when short‑term sentiment flips from “valuation worry” to “growth plus AI backlog.” 4) Trading/technical factors   Technically, EME has been in a strong bullish trend, trading above short‑, medium‑, and longer‑term moving averages with heavy volume spikes on up days. When you combine that with a high‑conviction fundamental story and pre‑earnings positioning, the stock is prone to gap moves as traders re‑risk into the AI/data center theme or cover shorts once the fundamental thesis is reaffirmed. 5) Very near‑term context   Ahead of the current earnings print, the stock had sold off on profit‑taking and valuation concerns, not on any fundamental deterioration. That kind of “pre‑earnings reset” often sets up a snap‑back gap if the company delivers another strong quarter or if guidance and backlog commentary reassure the market. Given EMCOR’s r
12365|ARM|2026-07-30|live|HIGH|60.0|10.77|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.00|08:55|ARM reported a record Q1 FY2027 earnings beat on July 29, with both revenues and EPS exceeding estimates, driven by 20%+ royalty growth and low-20s licensing revenue growth. Q2 guidance of $1.33B–$1.43B revenue straddled above the $1.339B consensus, and management disclosed that AGI CPU demand has surpassed $2B for FY2027–2028 — more than double prior disclosures — with a long-term $25B revenue ta|ARM's multi-quarter earnings inflection—record revenue beat, raised guidance, and AGI CPU demand doubling to $2B+—is an idiosyncratic company-specific acceleration, not part of a sector-wide theme; the AI chip demand surge is ARM's proprietary order-book inflection and competitive win, not a macro A|Fresh, primary-source-confirmed catalyst: a 6-K filed 2026-07-29 plus wire coverage of a record Q1 FY2027 with revenue-led growth (royalties +20%+, licensing low-20s) and Q2 guidance straddling above consensus — the load-bearing signal for a growth name is revenue acceleration, which is present, plus a doubled AGI CPU order book (>$2B FY27-28). This clears the HIGH bar, but the guidance only straddles consensus (not a clean raise) and much of the AGI CPU excitement is a long-dated 2030 narrative rather than a hard near-term beat, so I trim the floor's game_changer to strong. No active theme/na|ARM reported a record Q1 FY2027 earnings beat on July 29, with both revenues and EPS exceeding estimates, driven by 20%+ royalty growth and low-20s licensing revenue growth. Q2 guidance of $1.33B–$1.43B revenue straddled above the $1.339B consensus, and management disclosed that AGI CPU demand has surpassed $2B for FY2027–2028 — more than double prior disclosures — with a long-term $25B revenue target by 2030. For a $240B market cap AI infrastructure royalty platform, the combination of a record revenue beat, guidance raise, and a massive AGI CPU order book acceleration represents a strongly positive structural re-rating catalyst.|[SEC 6-K filed 2026-07-29, items ] arm-20260630 0001973239 ARM HOLDINGS PLC /UK 03-31 2026-06-30 2027 Q1 false 1 1 0 0 P3Y P6M 1 1 iso4217:USD iso4217:USD xbrli:shares xbrli:shares xbrli:pure iso4217:GBP arm:segment 0001973239 2026-04-01 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:NonrelatedPartyMember 2026-04-01 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:NonrelatedPartyMember 2025-04-01 2025-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:RelatedPartyMember 2026-04-01 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:RelatedPartyMember 2025-04-01 2025-06-30 0001973239 2025-04-01 2025-06-30 0001973239 2026-06-30 0001973239 2026-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:RelatedPartyMember 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:RelatedPartyMember 2026-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:CommonStockMember 2026-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:AdditionalPaidInCapitalMember 2026-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:AccumulatedOtherComprehensiveIncomeMember 2026-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:RetainedEarningsMember 2026-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:RetainedEarningsMember 2026-04-01 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:AccumulatedOtherComprehensiveIncomeMember 2026-04-01 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:AdditionalPaidInCapitalMember 2026-04-01 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:CommonStockMember 2026-04-01 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:CommonStockMember 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:AdditionalPaidInCapitalMember 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:AccumulatedOtherComprehensiveIncomeMember 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:RetainedEarningsMember 2026-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:CommonStockMember 2025-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:AdditionalPaidInCapitalMember 2025-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:AccumulatedOtherComprehensiveIncomeMember 2025-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:RetainedEarningsMember 2025-03-31 0001973239 2025-03-31 0001973239 us-gaap:RetainedEarningsMember 2025-04-01 2025-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:AccumulatedOtherComprehensiveIncomeMember 2025-04-01 2025-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:AdditionalPaidInCapitalMember 2025-04-01 2025-06-30 0001973239 us-gaap:CommonStockMember 2025-04-01 2025-06-30 0001973239 us-gaa