12373|BLZE|2026-07-31|live|HIGH|72.0|13.06|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|09:55|BLZE’s gap up was driven by the CoreWeave AI storage deal: Backblaze announced a multi-exabyte strategic agreement estimated at about $335 million, which the market read as a major validation of its role in AI infrastructure and a potential step-change in revenue visibility. The stock also got follow-through from analyst upgrades and bullish commentary after the deal, which helped extend the move.|CoreWeave's multi-exabyte storage agreement ($335M over 5-7 years, ~40% of BLZE market cap) is an idiosyncratic, company-specific multi-quarter revenue inflection and contract win—not a theme (not a sector-wide AI tailwind shared by peers), policy, or shortage driver, but a transformative deal that |The CoreWeave multi-exabyte storage agreement (~$335M over 5–7 years, ~40% of the $844M cap) is a transformative deal relative to company size and lands squarely in the live AI-infrastructure narrative, joining the active AI/data-center story even though BLZE isn't a listed cohort member. Deal specificity (named counterparty, dollar value, contract duration, physical infrastructure) plus follow-through upgrades supports a fundamental re-rating rather than a sector-drift gap. Caveat: has_direct_source=false — the catalyst rests on a web summary rather than a primary SEC/wire filing, so freshnes|The specific catalyst is a multi-exabyte strategic storage agreement with CoreWeave, valued at approximately $335M over 5–7 year terms — representing roughly 40% of Backblaze's current $844M market cap, a massive deal relative to company size. This is a fundamental re-rating event: the contract provides transformative revenue visibility, validates Backblaze as a serious AI infrastructure player, and was followed by analyst upgrades adding further momentum. The deal's scale, specificity (named counterparty, dollar value, contract duration, physical infrastructure details), and AI tailwind narrative make this a clear game-changer catalyst — not a routine move or sector-driven gap.|[Web summary] BLZE’s gap up was driven by the CoreWeave AI storage deal: Backblaze announced a multi-exabyte strategic agreement estimated at about $335 million, which the market read as a major validation of its role in AI infrastructure and a potential step-change in revenue visibility. The stock also got follow-through from analyst upgrades and bullish commentary after the deal, which helped extend the move.[3][2] The key catalyst was not a routine technical breakout; it was a fundamental re-rating on the CoreWeave partnership. Backblaze said the contract runs five and seven years on initial order forms, includes storage capacity from its data centers and managed storage inside CoreWeave facilities, and ties into AI-focused demand growth.[3] If you want, I can also break down whether the move was primarily a short squeeze, a momentum trade, or a valuation reset off the deal.
12374|FTK|2026-08-03|live|HIGH|60.0|10.45|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.00|08:45|The fresh catalyst (dated today, 2026-08-03) is Flotek's announcement of a 10-year partnership with Puerto Rico's electric utility (PREPA) to upgrade its natural-gas-powered grid, generating a ~$400M revenue backlog — an enormous contract relative to Flotek's scale (Q1 2026 annualized revenue ~$280M, and the company is a small-cap). At ~$40M in annual recurring revenue once fully ramped, this deal|Flotek's 10-year PREPA partnership is a company-specific multi-quarter revenue inflection—a $400M backlog and ~$40M ARR represent a transformative, idiosyncratic shift in the revenue trajectory unshared by peers, not a sector-wide theme or policy-driven subsidy.|Fresh, dated (2026-08-03) Benzinga wire confirms a 10-year PREPA power contract with a ~$400M revenue backlog (~$40M/yr recurring) — enormous versus Flotek's ~$280M annualized revenue and small-cap size, so it is transformative rather than incremental. Government-utility policy/infrastructure work plus 295% YoY Data Analytics growth makes this a structural pivot from challenged chemicals to power services, i.e. a genuine business-model re-rating, not boilerplate PR. It also rhymes with the active power/grid infrastructure narrative (EME/PWR), and the 10% gap on ~16x pre-market RVOL confirms th|The fresh catalyst (dated today, 2026-08-03) is Flotek's announcement of a 10-year partnership with Puerto Rico's electric utility (PREPA) to upgrade its natural-gas-powered grid, generating a ~$400M revenue backlog — an enormous contract relative to Flotek's scale (Q1 2026 annualized revenue ~$280M, and the company is a small-cap). At ~$40M in annual recurring revenue once fully ramped, this deal meaningfully expands the company's top-line trajectory and signals a transformative pivot from a challenged chemicals business toward a higher-value power services and data analytics platform. Combined with the company's existing 295% YoY Data Analytics growth and strong EBITDA momentum, this contract is a structural, company-defining catalyst that justifies a significant re-rating.|[Benzinga 2026-08-03] Flotek Signs 10-Year Puerto Rico Power Deal With $400M Backlog. [Web summary] The recent gap up in FTK (Flotek Industries) is being driven primarily by a major long-term contract announcement with Puerto Rico’s electric utility plus ongoing strength in its data analytics business and improving sentiment/coverage. Key latest catalyst: Flotek announced a decade-long partnership with the Puerto Rico Electric Power Authority (PREPA) to upgrade the island’s natural-gas-powered electric grid, with an expected revenue backlog of around $400 million from leasing gas-generation equipment and deploying its smart conditioning and distribution systems. This is a transformative-sized contract relative to Flotek’s current scale and is the dominant fundamental catalyst behind the recent move and re-rating in the stock. Supporting drivers and context: - The PREPA deal is expected to roll out starting in Q4 2026, with initial power generation systems by end of Q1 2027 and roughly $40 million in annual revenue once fully ramped. The market is pricing in that future revenue stream and associated margin leverage, which has driven a sharp rerating in the stock’s valuation multiples. - Flotek’s momentum score and 52-week performance are already strong, and the PREPA contract has reinforced a momentum/“growth story” narrative, contributing to follow-through buying and gap-type moves as investors update models. - Operationally, the company has been showing strong growth in its Data Analytics segment, which has become a much larger contributor to gross profit. Recent earnings showed nearly 300% year-over-year growth in Data Analytics revenue, with that segment representing about half of company gross profit, plus a sizable multi-year backlog tied to analytics and power services. This underpins the view that Flotek is transitioning from a challenged chemicals story to a higher-value analytics/power-services platform. - The company also extended the maturity of its revolving loan facility into late 2026, improving financing flexibility and reducing near-term balance sheet overhang, which helps support the stock on positive news. - On the market side, FTK has seen renewed attention ahead of its scheduled Q2 2026 earnings release (set for early August) and fresh analyst coverage, including a Buy initiation and aggressive upside targets. That combination of a clear upcoming event, strong recent price action, and a large new contract has boosted speculative interest and short-term trading activity, making a gap up more likely once the PREPA news became widely digested. In short, the PREPA long-term power contract is the core fundamental catalyst for FTK’s recent gap up, amplified by strong Data Analytics growth, improved credit flexibility, bullish analyst sentiment, and anticipation of the upcoming earnings print.
12375|LIND|2026-08-03|live|HIGH|70.0|15.55|strong|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.00|09:55|Lindblad reported Q2 2026 earnings with total revenue up 19% YoY to $199.2M, Adjusted EBITDA surging 31% to $32.5M with margin expansion, and occupancy hitting 91% — the strongest second-quarter occupancy in a decade — while simultaneously growing capacity 12%. The net yield per available guest night hit a record $1,294 (+4%), and the Land Experiences segment grew 23%. This is a broad-based beat a|Lindblad delivered a multi-metric beat (19% revenue growth, 31% EBITDA growth, record occupancy, record yield) demonstrating a company-specific inflection in pricing power and operational efficiency, with no evidence of sector-wide tailwind, policy support, or supply constraint—this is idiosyncratic|Fresh, primary-source catalyst: 8-K/EX-99.1 dated 2026-08-03 reporting Q2 revenue +19% YoY to $199.2M with Adjusted EBITDA +31%, record net yield $1,294 and decade-high 91% occupancy on 12% capacity growth — revenue-led acceleration with margin expansion, exactly the load-bearing signal, not an EPS-only or one-time flip. Materially meaningful for a $2.2B consumer cyclical, and the loss narrowing to $1.4M is corroborated by demand/yield inflection rather than an anomaly. No explicit guidance raise in the disclosed text and no theme/narrative cohort participation (unrelated to the active AI/data|Lindblad reported Q2 2026 earnings with total revenue up 19% YoY to $199.2M, Adjusted EBITDA surging 31% to $32.5M with margin expansion, and occupancy hitting 91% — the strongest second-quarter occupancy in a decade — while simultaneously growing capacity 12%. The net yield per available guest night hit a record $1,294 (+4%), and the Land Experiences segment grew 23%. This is a broad-based beat across revenue, EBITDA, yield, and occupancy, with the CEO expressing confidence in "sustainable long-term growth." However, the press release excerpt does not include an explicit forward guidance raise (the filing is truncated), which keeps this from reaching game_changer tier — though the combination of accelerating revenue growth, record metrics, and margin expansion on a $2.2B market cap consumer cyclical is a clearly strong, company-specific catalyst.|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-08-03, items 2.02,9.01] lindb20260528_8k.htm false 0001512499 0001512499 2026-08-03 2026-08-03 UNITED STATES SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION WASHINGTON, DC 20549 FORM 8-K CURRENT REPORT Pursuant to Section 13 or 15(d) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 Date of Report (Date of earliest event reported): August 3, 2026 LINDBLAD EXPEDITIONS HOLDINGS, INC. (Exact name of registrant as specified in its charter) Delaware 001-35898 27-4749725 (State or other jurisdiction of incorporation) (Commission File Number) (IRS Employer Identification No.) 11 West 42nd Street, Suite 22 B3 , New York , New York 10036 (Address of principal executive offices) (Zip Code) Registrant &#8217; s telephone number, including area code: ( 212 ) 261-9000 Securities registered pursuant to Section 12(b) of the Act: Title of each class Trading Symbol(s) Name of each exchange on which registered Common Stock , par value $0.0001 per share LIND The NASDAQ Stock Market LLC Not Applicable (Former name or former address, if changed since last report) Check the appropriate box below if the Form 8-K filing is intended to simultaneously satisfy the filing obligation of the registrant under any of the following provisions ( see General Instruction A.2. below): &#9744; Written communications pursuant to Rule 425 under the Securities Act (17 CFR 230.425) &#9744; Soliciting material pursuant to Rule 14a-12 under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14a-12) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 14d-2(b) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14d-2(b)) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 13e-4(c) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.13e-4(c)) Indicate by check mark whether the registrant is an emerging growth co