12385|INSP|2026-08-04|live|MODERATE|55.0|22.29|routine|MODERATE|judge|policy|1.00|09:45|INSP reported Q2 2026 revenue of $200.6 million, which represents a 7.6% year-over-year DECLINE, driven by a drop in U.S. revenue amid an evolving coding and reimbursement environment. While the company raised 2026 guidance and announced a $30M cost redeployment "strategic growth plan," the top-line revenue contraction is a fundamental negative for a growth-oriented medtech name. Adjusted diluted |The gap-up catalyst is driven by an evolving coding and reimbursement environment—a regulatory/policy headwind affecting U.S. revenue—combined with forward guidance raise and cost-redeployment strategy, positioning management's proactive response to a policy-driven revenue challenge as a stabilizing|The catalyst is fresh and real (8-K/press release dated 2026-08-03 with Q2 results, raised 2026 guidance, and a $30M cost-redeployment plan), so it clears the freshness bar, but revenue DECLINED 7.6% Y/Y on U.S. reimbursement headwinds — the load-bearing signal for a growth medtech is revenue acceleration, and it is negative here. Adjusted EPS of $0.14 with $0.01 GAAP is not a sustainable turnaround inflection, and a $30M expense reallocation on a $1.8B cap is a minor, self-help item rather than a transformative event. No theme or narrative axis lights: this is an idiosyncratic sleep-apnea rei|INSP reported Q2 2026 revenue of $200.6 million, which represents a 7.6% year-over-year DECLINE, driven by a drop in U.S. revenue amid an evolving coding and reimbursement environment. While the company raised 2026 guidance and announced a $30M cost redeployment "strategic growth plan," the top-line revenue contraction is a fundamental negative for a growth-oriented medtech name. Adjusted diluted EPS of $0.14 is minimal and not a turnaround signal. A guidance raise on top of declining revenue, in a challenging reimbursement environment, does not meet the bar for "strong" or "game_changer" on a growth stock.|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-08-03, items 2.02,7.01,9.01] insp-20260803 0001609550 False 0001609550 2026-08-03 2026-08-03 UNITED STATES SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION Washington, D.C. 20549 _________________________ FORM 8-K _________________________ CURRENT REPORT Pursuant to Section 13 or 15(d) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 Date of report (Date of earliest event reported): August 3, 2026 _________________________ INSPIRE MEDICAL SYSTEMS, INC. (Exact name of registrant as specified in its charter) _________________________ Delaware 001-38468 26-1377674 (State or other jurisdiction of incorporation) (Commission File Number) (I.R.S. Employer Identification No.) 5500 Wayzata Blvd. , Suite 1600 Golden Valley , Minnesota 55416 (Address of principal executive offices) (Zip Code) ( 844 ) 672-4357 (Registrant&#8217;s telephone number, including area code) N/A (Former name or former address, if changed since last report) Check the appropriate box below if the Form 8-K filing is intended to simultaneously satisfy the filing obligation of the registrant under any of the following provisions: &#9744; Written communications pursuant to Rule 425 under the Securities Act (17 CFR 230.425) &#9744; Soliciting material pursuant to Rule 14a-12 under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14a-12) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 14d-2(b) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14d-2(b)) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 13e-4(c) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.13e-4(c)) Securities registered pursuant to Section 12(b) of the Act: Title of each class Trading Symbol(s) Name of each exchange on which registered Common Stock, $0.001 par value per share INSP New York Stock Exchange Indicate by check mark whether the registrant is an emerging growth company as defined in Rule 405 of the Securities Act of 1933 (&#167;230.405 of this chapter) or Rule 12b-2 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (&#167;240.12b-2 of this chapter). Emerging growth company &#9744; If an emerging growth company, indicate by check mark if the registrant has elected not to use the extended transition period for complying with any new or revised financial accounting standards provided pursuant to Section 13(a) of the Exchange Act. &#9744; Item 2.02. Results of Operations and Financial Condition. On August 3, 2026, Inspire Medical Systems, Inc. (the &#8220;Company&#8221;) issued a press release announcing its financial results for the quarter ended June 30, 2026. The full text of the press release is furnished as Exhibit 99.1 to this Current Report on Form 8-K and is incorporated herein by reference. Item 7.01. Regulation FD Disclosure. In August and September of 2026, the Company will be participating in various meetings with investors and analysts, and a copy of the Company&#8217;s presentation materials being used at these meetings is furnished as Exhibit 99.2 hereto and is incorporated herein by reference. These presentation materials are also available on t
12386|TSAT|2026-08-04|live|HIGH|60.0|14.46|game_changer|HIGH|judge|policy|1.00|09:55|Telesat announced a freshly signed $2.3B CAD (up to $2.7B with options) 15-year military satcom contract with Canada's Defence Investment Agency — the largest contract in the company's six-decade history. Against a market cap of ~$2.1B USD, this single contract is transformative in scale, immediately expanding the Telesat Lightspeed constellation by 44% (156 → 225 satellites) with fully funded mil|A freshly signed federal/defense contract award from Canada's Defence Investment Agency is a government-regulatory driver meeting the policy definition; it is company-specific (not a theme or sector-wide tailwind) and concrete with named counterparty, specific dollar figures, and structured mileston|SEC 6-K filed today (Aug 4, 2026) with the press release attached confirms a freshly signed $2.3B CAD (up to $2.7B) 15-year military satcom contract with Canada's Defence Investment Agency — the largest in Telesat's history, ~equal to its entire $2.4B market cap, so materiality is unambiguously transformative. It is fully funded with milestone payments starting Q3 2026 and expands the Lightspeed constellation 44% (156→225 satellites), giving structural revenue visibility rather than a vague pipeline claim. Government-policy/defense procurement sits high in the Pradeep hierarchy and the primary|Telesat announced a freshly signed $2.3B CAD (up to $2.7B with options) 15-year military satcom contract with Canada's Defence Investment Agency — the largest contract in the company's six-decade history. Against a market cap of ~$2.1B USD, this single contract is transformative in scale, immediately expanding the Telesat Lightspeed constellation by 44% (156 → 225 satellites) with fully funded milestone payments beginning Q3 2026, accelerating the path to commercial service in Q1 2028. The contract is concrete, freshly disclosed via SEC 6-K filed today (August 4, 2026), with specific dollar figures, a named counterparty, and structural revenue visibility — this is a genuine, verifiable, company-specific catalyst of the highest magnitude relative to Telesat's size.|[SEC 6-K filed 2026-08-04, items ] EX-99.1 2 ea030033301ex99-1.htm PRESS RELEASE DATED AUGUST 4, 2026 - "TELESAT SECURES $2.3 BILLION ARCTIC MILITARY SATCOM CONTRACT, EXPANDING TELESAT LIGHTSPEED NETWORK AND CAPACITY BY 44%" Exhibit 99.1 Telesat secures $2.3 billion Arctic military satcom contract, expanding Telesat Lightspeed network and capacity by 44% Largest contract in company&rsquo;s six-decade history expands Telesat Lightspeed constellation to 225 satellites and delivers secure Mil-Ka Arctic connectivity for the Canadian Armed Forces OTTAWA, CANADA &ndash; August 4, 2026 &ndash; Telesat LEO ULC, a subsidiary of Telesat Corporation (Nasdaq and TSX: TSAT), one of the world&rsquo;s largest and most innovative satellite operators, today announced that it has signed a $2.3 billion Telesat Lightspeed services contract with Canada&rsquo;s Defence Investment Agency (DIA) to deliver secure Military Ka-band (Mil-Ka) Arctic connectivity to the Canadian Armed Forces (CAF) under the Enhanced Satellite Communications Project &ndash; Polar (ESCP-P) program. The contract also includes two five-year option periods valued at approximately $200 million each, bringing the total contract value to $2.7 billion. All amounts are in Canadian dollars unless otherwise noted. The contract is for a period of 15 years of service, including the option years, with service under the contract beginning in 2028. The expected start of Telesat Lightspeed global service delivery remains on track for Q1 2028. This is the largest contract in Telesat&rsquo;s history and will significantly expand the scale and capacity of the Telesat Lightspeed network, positioning the company for accelerated growth. This agreement allows Telesat to immediately expand its Telesat Lightspeed network by 69 fully funded satellites, bringing the initial constellation from 156 to 225 satellites, all to be built by MDA Space, with both commercial and Mil-Ka capacity. The expansion will be funded through milestone-based payments from the Government of Canada starting in Q3 2026. &ldquo;This contract ensures the Canadian Armed Forces will have access to secure, resilient and sovereign connectivity years earlier than originally planned, at a great value for money, and with a scalable architecture designed to meet future global operational requirements and support interoperability with Canada&rsquo;s allies and partners,&rdquo; said Dan Goldberg, Telesat&rsquo;s President and CEO. &ldquo;High speed and low latency connectivity is critical for modern military forces, and Telesat Lightspeed is expected to be foundational for future CAF and allied governments&rsquo; operations.&rdquo; Goldberg added, &ldquo;This contract also represents a step change in the scale and commercial potential of Telesat Lightspeed. The significant expansion of our constellation, combined with burgeoning demand for secure satellite communications worldwide, positions us to accelerate the growth of our business while serving the evo
12387|APPS|2026-08-05|live|HIGH|115.2|28.29|strong|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|07:00|Digital Turbine reported fiscal Q1 2027 earnings with revenue of $166.0M (+27% YoY), Non-GAAP Adjusted EBITDA of $42.5M (+69% YoY), and Non-GAAP EPS of $0.19 vs. $0.06 in the prior year period — all meaningfully above trend. Critically, management raised full-year guidance alongside the beat, which is the key momentum trigger. The combination of strong double-digit revenue acceleration, a near-tri|Digital Turbine demonstrated a multi-quarter idiosyncratic earnings inflection with revenue +27% YoY, Non-GAAP EPS nearly tripling (+217%), and management raising full-year guidance—a classic beat-and-raise signaling genuine company-specific acceleration, not a sector theme, policy driver, or struct|Fresh, primary-source catalyst: 8-K/EX-99.1 dated 2026-08-04 shows revenue +27% YoY to $166.0M with the App Growth Platform segment +56% and adjusted EBITDA +69%, plus an explicit full-year guidance RAISE — revenue acceleration with a raise is exactly the load-bearing signal for a growth name. Materially significant for a $1.2B cap, and the 28% gap on 37x pre-market RVOL confirms the market repricing the growth trajectory, not sector drift. Held at strong rather than game_changer because the company is still GAAP-unprofitable and the magnitude of the guidance lift isn't quantified in the excer|Digital Turbine reported fiscal Q1 2027 earnings with revenue of $166.0M (+27% YoY), Non-GAAP Adjusted EBITDA of $42.5M (+69% YoY), and Non-GAAP EPS of $0.19 vs. $0.06 in the prior year period — all meaningfully above trend. Critically, management raised full-year guidance alongside the beat, which is the key momentum trigger. The combination of strong double-digit revenue acceleration, a near-tripling of non-GAAP EPS, and a guidance raise on a $1.2B market-cap growth name is a clear, fresh, verifiable catalyst that warrants a "strong" classification; it falls short of "game_changer" given the company remains GAAP-unprofitable (GAAP net loss of $3.2M) and the raise details were not fully excerpted, leaving magnitude of the guidance lift uncertain.|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-08-04, items 2.02,9.01] apps-20260804 false 0000317788 110 San Antonio Street, Austin, Suite 160, TX 0000317788 2026-08-04 2026-08-04 UNITED STATES SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION WASHINGTON, DC 20549 FORM 8-K CURRENT REPORT PURSUANT TO SECTION 13 OR 15(d) OF THE SECURITIES EXCHANGE ACT OF 1934 Date of Report (date of earliest event reported): August 4, 2026 Digital Turbine, Inc. (Exact Name of Registrant as Specified in Its Charter) Delaware 001-35958 22-2267658 (State or Other Jurisdiction of Incorporation) (Commission File Number) (IRS Employer Identification No.) 110 San Antonio Street, Suite 160, Austin, TX 78701 (Address of Principal Executive Offices) (Zip Code) ( 512 ) 387-7717 (Registrant&#8217;s Telephone Number, Including Area Code) (Former Name or Former Address, if Changed Since Last Report) Check the appropriate box below if the Form 8-K filing is intended to simultaneously satisfy the filing obligation of the registrant under any of the following provisions. ( see General Instruction A.2. below) &#9744; Written communications pursuant to Rule 425 under the Securities Act (17 CFR 230.425) &#9744; Soliciting material pursuant to Rule 14a-12 under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14a-12) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 14d-2(b) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14d-2(b)) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 13e-4(c) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.13e-4(c)) Securities registered pursuant to Section 12(b) of the Act: Title of each class Trading symbol(s) Name of each exchange on which registered Common Stock APPS NASDAQ Indicate by check mark whether the registrant is an emerging growth company as defined in Rule 405 of the Securities Act of 1933 (&#167;230.405 of this chapter) or Rule 12b-2 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (&#167;2