12394|ROCK|2026-08-05|live|MODERATE|60.5|14.90|strong|MODERATE|judge|other|1.20|09:50|Gibraltar Industries reported Q2 2026 earnings (8-K Item 2.02, filed August 5, 2026) with net sales +65% (largely acquisition-driven via OmniMax, not organic) and organic growth of only +5%, with Adjusted EPS of $1.11. Critically, the company merely reiterated its full-year 2026 guidance rather than raising it — on a growth/industrials name, a guidance reiteration (not a raise) with modest organic|Gibraltar reported in-line earnings with +65% headline revenue (acquisition-inflated, not organic), only +5% organic growth, and guidance reiteration (not a raise); absent a beat-and-raise or idiosyncratic inflection, this is pedestrian company news lacking a structural pivot catalyst.|Fresh, primary-sourced catalyst exists (8-K Item 2.02 + EX-99.1 dated 2026-08-05), so the driver is identified — but its content is weak: the +65% net sales headline is acquisition-inflated by OmniMax while organic growth is only ~5%, and full-year guidance was merely reiterated, not raised. Per the earnings rule, revenue acceleration with a guidance raise is the load-bearing signal on a non-turnaround industrials name; an adjusted-EPS print with M&A-driven optics and no raise does not qualify for HIGH. No active theme or narrative-cohort linkage (residential building products is unrelated to |Gibraltar Industries reported Q2 2026 earnings (8-K Item 2.02, filed August 5, 2026) with net sales +65% (largely acquisition-driven via OmniMax, not organic) and organic growth of only +5%, with Adjusted EPS of $1.11. Critically, the company merely reiterated its full-year 2026 guidance rather than raising it — on a growth/industrials name, a guidance reiteration (not a raise) with modest organic growth is not a meaningful positive catalyst. The headline revenue surge is M&A-inflated and does not reflect accelerating organic momentum, and the absence of a guidance raise removes the key ingredient needed for a "strong