12432|FRMI|2026-08-11|live|HIGH|96.0|17.01|game_changer|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.00|07:00|Fresh, concrete catalyst dated today (Benzinga 2026-08-10/11): Fermi signed its first binding 15-year lease with TensorWave for a 222MW AI data center at Project Matador, worth ~$6.5B in contracted revenue, with expansion rights to 650MW+. This is a company-defining, first-of-its-kind commercial contract that is roughly 1.7x the company's entire $3.8B market cap, providing long-term contracted rev|First binding 15-year, $6.5B contracted revenue lease with TensorWave for 222MW AI data center represents a company-specific, transformative sales inflection and multi-quarter earnings visibility driver idiosyncratic to Fermi, not a shared theme, policy, or shortage.|Fresh, dated (2026-08-10) wire-reported catalyst: first binding 15-year TensorWave lease for 222MW worth ~$6.5B in contracted revenue — ~1.7x the $3.8B market cap — with expansion rights to 650MW+, which validates the entire pre-revenue business model rather than incrementally adding to it. This is a company-defining, name-specific commercial contract, the strongest form of sales acceleration/de-risking for a no-revenue developer, and it plugs directly into the live AI-infrastructure buildout story even though FRMI sits in a different (power/real-estate) layer than the listed chip-enablement c|Fresh, concrete catalyst dated today (Benzinga 2026-08-10/11): Fermi signed its first binding 15-year lease with TensorWave for a 222MW AI data center at Project Matador, worth ~$6.5B in contracted revenue, with expansion rights to 650MW+. This is a company-defining, first-of-its-kind commercial contract that is roughly 1.7x the company's entire $3.8B market cap, providing long-term contracted revenue visibility and validating the business model for gigawatt-scale AI power infrastructure. This is a transformative, name-specific contract catalyst (not stale financing/dilution news or vague sector momentum), warranting game_changer classification.|[Benzinga 2026-08-10] Fermi Signs First Binding 15-Year TensorWave Lease Worth $6.5B For 222MW AI Data Center, With Expansion Rights To More Than 650MW. [Web summary] FRMI gapped up on news that it signed its first binding customer lease at Project Matador in Texas with AI cloud provider TensorWave. The deal is for a 15-year turnkey data-center lease backed by 222 MW of power and is expected to generate about $6.5 billion in contracted revenue, which is the clearest near-term catalyst behind the move.[3][5][6] The market also appears to be trading the stock on renewed AI-infrastructure optimism after prior weakness, including July’s selloff tied to capital-raise and dilution concerns.[1][4][9] If you want, I can also give you the intraday price action context, whether the move was a continuation from the OpenAI-buzz rally, and what the next likely catalysts are into earnings.
12433|SE|2026-08-11|live|HIGH|54.7|10.58|strong|HIGH|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|07:25|Today's fresh catalyst is Sea Limited's Q2 2026 earnings release: EPS of $0.70 missed the $0.75 estimate, but revenue of $7.788B beat the $7.063B estimate by a wide margin (~10%), indicating accelerating top-line growth across e-commerce (Shopee), digital financial services, and gaming. Per the grading rules, on a growth name like SE, revenue beat/acceleration matters more than an EPS miss, and a |Sea Limited posted a ~10% revenue beat ($7.788B vs. $7.063B estimate) with accelerating top-line growth across multiple segments (Shopee e-commerce, digital financial services, gaming), representing a company-specific multi-quarter earnings inflection idiosyncratic to SE, not a sector-wide theme or |Fresh dated earnings print (Benzinga 2026-08-11) shows revenue of $7.788B vs $7.063B expected — a ~10% top-line beat driven by Shopee GMV, fintech and gaming, which is the load-bearing signal on a growth name and outweighs the modest EPS miss. Gap of ~10.6% on 35x pre-market RVOL aligns cleanly with the print, so the driver is identified and material for a company of SE's scale. No active theme/narrative membership (AI-chip cohort is unrelated) and no explicit guidance raise, so it stays strong rather than game_changer; the Aug-4 PT cut is stale background.|Today's fresh catalyst is Sea Limited's Q2 2026 earnings release: EPS of $0.70 missed the $0.75 estimate, but revenue of $7.788B beat the $7.063B estimate by a wide margin (~10%), indicating accelerating top-line growth across e-commerce (Shopee), digital financial services, and gaming. Per the grading rules, on a growth name like SE, revenue beat/acceleration matters more than an EPS miss, and a strong revenue beat of this magnitude qualifies as a strong catalyst even without an explicit guidance raise mentioned. The TD Cowen price target cut (Aug 4, stale/pre-existing) is background context, not today's driver.|[Benzinga 2026-08-11] Sea Q2 EPS $0.70 Misses $0.75 Estimate, Sales $7.788B Beat $7.063B Estimate. [Benzinga 2026-08-04] TD Cowen Maintains Hold on Sea, Lowers Price Target to $100. [Web summary] SE likely gapped up on a combination of earnings-related momentum and traders positioning ahead of Sea Limited’s next catalyst, with the stock’s recent attention tied to accelerated revenue growth in e-commerce, digital financial services, and gaming, plus a top-line beat from strong Shopee GMV growth in the latest earnings snapshot.[2][11] The most immediate scheduled catalyst is Sea Limited’s Q2 2026 earnings report, which is due pre-market on August 13, and that timing alone can drive a pre-earnings gap as traders front-run the print.[12] The company has also been in focus because Sea has been posting strong growth but with tighter margins from heavier investment in logistics, user acquisition, and credit growth, which keeps it very much a momentum-and-catalyst stock.[2] If you want, I can also give you the exact price-moving headline for today’s gap, if there was a specific premarket news item or analyst action.
12434|RPD|2026-08-11|live|none|84.0|16.78|strong|none|judge|other|1.00|09:50|Rapid7 reported Q2 2026 results: revenue of $210.9M, described as a "decrease" year-over-year, alongside ARR of $824M. This is a revenue decline, not growth or acceleration, paired with a restructuring plan (12% workforce cut) aimed at cost-cutting rather than a growth inflection. There is no guidance raise, no beat-and-raise dynamic, and no structural EPS turnaround disclosed — non-GAAP operating|Declining revenue year-over-year with a defensive cost-cutting restructuring (12% workforce reduction) and no guidance raise or earnings inflection constitutes pedestrian, in-line or negative company news—not a positive sales acceleration, policy driver, theme catalyst, or structural inflection.|The verifiable fresh catalyst (8-K/EX-99.1, Aug 10 2026) is a Q2 print with revenue DECLINING year-over-year ($210.9M) plus a defensive 12% workforce restructuring — cost-cutting, not the revenue growth/acceleration or guidance raise that the market pays for on a software name. Non-GAAP operating income of $28.9M is margin expansion via cuts, not a structural demand or turnaround inflection, so EPS is not load-bearing here. Cybersecurity is not the active AI-chip-enablement narrative, so no theme/narrative axis lights; demote from the HIGH floor to routine.|Rapid7 reported Q2 2026 results: revenue of $210.9M, described as a "decrease" year-over-year, alongside ARR of $824M. This is a revenue decline, not growth or acceleration, paired with a restructuring plan (12% workforce cut) aimed at cost-cutting rather than a growth inflection. There is no guidance raise, no beat-and-raise dynamic, and no structural EPS turnaround disclosed — non-GAAP operating income of $28.9M is presented without context of a sustainable inflection. Given declining revenue and a defensive restructuring announcement, this does not meet the bar for strong/game_changer; it is a routine (or negative) event, not a genuine EP momentum catalyst.|[SEC 8-K filed 2026-08-10, items 2.02,2.05,9.01] rp-20260807 0001560327 false 0001560327 2026-05-05 2026-05-05 UNITED STATES SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION Washington, D.C. 20549 FORM 8-K CURRENT REPORT Pursuant to Section 13 or 15(d) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 Date of Report (Date of earliest event reported): August 7, 2026 Rapid7, Inc. (Exact name of registrant as specified in its charter) Delaware 001-37496 35-2423994 (State or other jurisdiction of incorporation) (Commission File Number) (IRS Employer Identification No.) 120 Causeway Street, Boston, Massachusetts 02114 (Address of principal executive offices), including zip code ( 617 ) 247-1717 (Registrant&#8217;s telephone number, including area code) Not Applicable (Former name, or former address, if changed since last report) Check the appropriate box below if the Form 8-K filing is intended to simultaneously satisfy the filing obligation of the registrant under any of the following provisions: &#9744; Written communications pursuant to Rule 425 under the Securities Act (17 CFR 230.425) &#9744; Soliciting material pursuant to Rule 14a-12 under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14a-12) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 14d-2(b) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.14d-2(b)) &#9744; Pre-commencement communications pursuant to Rule 13e-4(c) under the Exchange Act (17 CFR 240.13e-4(c)) Securities registered pursuant to Section 12(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934: Title of each class Trading symbol(s) Name of each exchange on which registered Common Stock, $0.01 par value per share RPD The Nasdaq Global Market Indicate by check mark whether the registrant is an emerging growth company as defined in as defined in Rule 405 of the Securities Act of 1933 (&#167;230.405 of this chapter) or Rule 12b-2 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (&#167;240.12b-2 of this chapter). Emerging growth company &#9744; If an emerging growth company, indicate by check mark if the regis