12441|HRB|2026-08-12|live|MODERATE|100.8|16.76|strong|MODERATE|judge|sales_acceleration|1.20|09:45|HRB reported fiscal 2026 (FY ended June 30, 2026) results with revenue up 4.9% to $3.95B, net income from continuing operations up 20.8% to $736.3M, and EPS from continuing operations up 28.7% to $5.69 (though partly aided by a one-time tax benefit in Q3). Operating cash flow rose 23%, the company returned $714M to shareholders, and the board raised the quarterly dividend by 10% to $0.46/share - a|HRB reported a multi-quarter earnings inflection with 28.7% EPS growth and 23% operating cash flow growth, both idiosyncratic beats (not a sector theme, policy, or shortage driver), anchored by a freshly-announced, concrete earnings result rather than forward guidance or pre-catalyst anticipation.|The catalyst is real and freshly dated (8-K + wire, 8/11/2026), but it is a mature $7B tax-prep name posting only ~4.9% annual revenue growth — the EPS gain (+28.7%) is flattened by a one-time non-cash tax benefit, exactly the CBRL-class non-structural inflection the rubric discounts. A 10% dividend hike and $714M buyback/dividend return are ordinary capital-return housekeeping for a cash-cow business, not a demand or margin inflection that changes the earnings trajectory. No theme or narrative cohort participation (no AI/infra story) and no direct-source deal to promote on, so the fresh-but-i|HRB reported fiscal 2026 (FY ended June 30, 2026) results with revenue up 4.9% to $3.95B, net income from continuing operations up 20.8% to $736.3M, and EPS from continuing operations up 28.7% to $5.69 (though partly aided by a one-time tax benefit in Q3). Operating cash flow rose 23%, the company returned $714M to shareholders, and the board raised the quarterly dividend by 10% to $0.46/share - a concrete, freshly-dated (8/11/2026) earnings beat plus dividend hike, which is a solid combination of fundamental and capital-return catalysts for a mature consumer cyclical name, though not transfor